  "discovery_metadata": {
    "doi": "10.5796/electrochemistry.25-71119",
    "title": "Preparation of Biomass-Derived Carbon Materials via Molten Salt Method and Their Supercapacitor Performance",
    "score": 12,
    "date": "2025-09-11",
    "abstract": "Biomass (corn cob) derived porous carbon with a very high surface area for supercapacitor application is reported by carbonization with a non-toxic mixed salt of NaCl and KCl as the reaction medium.The corn cob was subjected to molten salt carbonization in a molten NaCl-KCl system (mass ratio of 1:5) at 800 °C, porous carbon material (C-1-5) with a high specific surface area (1054.36m² g⁻¹) and a threedimensional honeycomb network structure was obtained.The electrode material exhibits a specific capacitance of 222.5 F g⁻¹ in a three-electrode system at a current density of 0.1 A g⁻¹.The assembled symmetric supercapacitor achieves a specific capacitance of 76.47 F g⁻¹ at a scan rate of 5 mV s⁻¹.The energy density is 6.22 Wh kg⁻¹ at a power density of 50 W kg⁻¹, and the capacitance retention reaches 96.30% after 2000 cycles.This green and simple technology is expected to become an effective and scalable method for producing porous carbon from biomass for electrochemical energy storage applications.",
